## 4. Hands-on Drills

### Drill 1: Ceph RBD Dynamic Filesystem Volume

#### Step 1: Deploy Claim & Consumer Pod

```bash
cd {{ cka_lab_volumes_dir }}/06-ceph

kubectl apply -f 01-rbd-dynamic-filesystem.yaml
kubectl wait --for=condition=Ready pod/rbd-fs-consumer-pod --timeout=120s
```

#### Step 2: Verify Storage Attachment

```bash
kubectl get pvc rbd-claim-fs
kubectl get pv
kubectl exec rbd-fs-consumer-pod -- df -h /mnt/storage
kubectl exec rbd-fs-consumer-pod -- cat /mnt/storage/data.txt
```

---

### Drill 2: Ceph RBD Raw Block Device Mode (`volumeMode: Block`)

In high-performance database workloads, containers consume unformatted block devices without a host filesystem layer.

#### Step 1: Deploy Raw Block PVC & Consumer Pod

```bash
kubectl apply -f 02-rbd-raw-block.yaml
kubectl wait --for=condition=Ready pod/rbd-raw-block-consumer-pod --timeout=120s
```

#### Step 2: Verify Raw Block Device Inside Container

```bash
kubectl exec rbd-raw-block-consumer-pod -- ls -l /dev/rbdblock
# Test raw block I/O write with dd:
kubectl exec rbd-raw-block-consumer-pod -- dd if=/dev/urandom of=/dev/rbdblock bs=1M count=10 conv=fsync
```

---

### Drill 3: Ceph RBD Online Live Volume Expansion

Because `{{ cka_lab_rbd_storage_class | default('rook-ceph-block') }}` has `allowVolumeExpansion: true`, the CSI resizer
expands the volume and
kubelet resizes the ext4 filesystem live without pod restart:

#### Step 1: Patch the PVC Capacity to 2Gi

```bash
kubectl patch pvc rbd-claim-fs -p '{"spec":{"resources":{"requests":{"storage":"2Gi"}}}}'
kubectl get pvc rbd-claim-fs
```

#### Step 2: Confirm Filesystem Resizing Inside the Running Pod

```bash
kubectl exec rbd-fs-consumer-pod -- df -h /mnt/storage
```

---

### Drill 4: Native CephFS Dynamic Shared Storage (`ReadWriteMany`)

Native CephFS provides distributed POSIX shared storage across multiple cluster nodes via `cephfs.csi.ceph.com`:

#### Step 1: Deploy StorageClass and RWX Claim

```bash
kubectl apply -f 05-cephfs-sc.yaml
kubectl apply -f 06-cephfs-dynamic-pvc.yaml
kubectl get pvc cephfs-dynamic-claim
```

#### Step 2: Mount Across Multi-Replica Workload

```bash
kubectl apply -f 07-cephfs-shared-deployment.yaml
kubectl rollout status deployment/cephfs-shared-app --timeout=120s
kubectl get pods -l app=cephfs-shared-app -o wide
```

#### Step 3: Verify Distributed Read/Write

```bash
POD1=$(kubectl get pods -l app=cephfs-shared-app -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')
POD2=$(kubectl get pods -l app=cephfs-shared-app -o jsonpath='{.items[1].metadata.name}')

kubectl exec "${POD1}" -- cat /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html
kubectl exec "${POD2}" -- cat /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html
```

---

### Drill 5: Ceph NFS via NFS-Ganesha & NFS CSI (`ReadWriteMany`)

In enterprise environments, CephFS is often exported over standard NFSv4 via Ceph NFS-Ganesha daemons, allowing standard
NFS clients and the official Kubernetes NFS CSI driver (`nfs.csi.k8s.io`) to mount Ceph storage:

#### Step 1: Deploy Ceph NFS StorageClass and Claim

```bash
kubectl apply -f 08-ceph-nfs-sc.yaml
kubectl apply -f 09-ceph-nfs-dynamic-pvc.yaml
kubectl get pvc ceph-nfs-dynamic-claim
```

#### Step 2: Deploy Shared Workload

```bash
kubectl apply -f 10-ceph-nfs-shared-deployment.yaml
kubectl rollout status deployment/ceph-nfs-shared-app --timeout=120s
```

